* RSpice op-amp macromodels (single-pole, ideal input offset of 0 V).
* X1 inp inn out opamp  → A0 = 1e5, pole at ~10 Hz, Rout = 50 Ω.

.subckt opamp inp inn out
Rin inp inn 1meg
G1 0 n1 inp inn 1
R1 n1 0 1
C1 n1 0 15.9m
E1 out 0 n1 0 1e5
Rout out 0 50
.ends opamp

* Unity-gain-stable variant with a lower pole and 75 Ω output.
.subckt opamp_u inp inn out
Rin inp inn 10meg
G1 0 n1 inp inn 1
R1 n1 0 1
C1 n1 0 31.8m
E1 out 0 n1 0 2e5
Rout out 0 75
.ends opamp_u

* Comparator: high gain, no compensation.
.subckt comparator inp inn out PARAMS: gain=1e6 rout=100
Rin inp inn 1meg
E1 out 0 inp inn {gain}
Rout out 0 {rout}
.ends comparator

* Two-pole macromodel, gm-RC two-stage chain (documented interpretation:
* the response is the exact two-pole map built here, not a fitted vendor
* curve). Stage 1: G1 = A0 S into R1·C1 puts the dominant pole at
* f1 = 1/(2π·R1·C1); stage 2 repeats the chain (G2·R2 = 1, second pole at
* f2 = 1/(2π·R2·C2)) and E1 drives the 50 Ω output. Defaults: A0 = 1e5,
* f1 = 10 Hz → GBW = 1 MHz, f2 = 400 kHz — at a closed-loop gain of 100
* the crossover sits at 10 kHz with ≈76° phase margin.
.subckt opamp2 inp inn out PARAMS: gain=1e5 r1=1 c1=15.9m c2=3.979u rout=50
Rin inp inn 1meg
G1 0 n1 inp inn {gain}
R1 n1 0 {r1}
C1 n1 0 {c1}
G2 0 n2 n1 0 1
R2 n2 0 1
C2 n2 0 {c2}
E1 out 0 n2 0 1
Rout out 0 {rout}
.ends opamp2
